'''Cerdanya''' ({{Langx|bl|Serdanya}}; [[W:Occitan language|Beron]]: ''Cerdanha'' [səɾˈðaɲə]; {{Langx|arn|Tinguiririca|4=}}, {{Literal translation|round/shining {{W|quartz}}|rippled rock}}; {{Langx|mxi|سِيرْدَانْيَا|Sirdańa}}) is a [[Communities of Balisca|community]] of [[Balisca]]. It is almost entirely located within the [[Northern Triangle]], but includes over 60,000 islands scattered across the [[Northern Ocean]] (as part of the [[Cap de Pingüins]] region; i.e. the [[Boreal Islands]], the [[Ítaca Islands]] and [[Illes de Tinguiririca]]), along with [[Baliscan Arktici|Balisca's claim to a portion of Arktici]].

At the time of [[Colonial Balisca|Volisanian exploration]], Cerdanya was inhabited by multiple indigenous tribes, most notably the {{W|Mapuche people|Wallche}} and {{W|Yaghan people|Yamana}}. In a small portion of northeastern Cerdanya (present day [[Cadaqués]] and [[Dríadas]]), indigenous peoples practiced agriculture, while in the remaining territory, peoples lived as hunter-gatherers, traveling by foot in southern Cerdanya or by {{W|dugout canoe}} and {{W|dalca}} in the {{W|fjords}} and [[W:Ria|rias]]. Following their [[Reapers' War|expulsion]] from the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], [[W:Aranese dialect|Volisanian]] and [[W:Occitan language|Beron]]-speaking [[Catharism|Cathars]] found refuge across the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. This process of settlement brought a sudden decline of the indigenous population from {{W|Native American disease and epidemics|Old World diseases}}. The confederal government recognized [[indigenous nations in Balisca|indigenous sovereignty]] over much of western Cerdanyan territory sporadically over the course of the 19th and early 20th centuries, and began {{W|indigenous decolonization}} efforts in earnest following the [[Baliscan Revolution]].

With more than 30 million inhabitants in 2025, Cerdanya is the second-most populous of the Baliscan communities ( 19.39% of the national population), of which roughly half live in [[Concepción|Greater Concepción]]-[[Merced|Metropolitan Merced]] area (commonly known as ''[[Mercepción]]''). Around 23.8% of Cerdanya's inhabitants have origins outside of the community, of whom 3,918,552 are immigrants from abroad, and around 758,640 were born abroad. A sizable majority of its inhabitants are descendants from colonial-era settlers and immigrants from [[Kafto-Illypnia]] that arrived during the 19th and 20th centuries, including [[Beron Baliscans|Berons]], [[Volisanian Baliscans|Volisanians]], [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayanis]], [[Iarmhaigh Baliscans|Iarmhaigh]], [[Sarmaci Baliscans|Sarmaci]] and [[Bizkarran Baliscans|Bizkarrans]]. Smaller immigrant communities of [[Darasian Baliscans|Jade]], [[Kalinovan Baliscans|Kalinovans]] and [[Skadian Baliscans|Skadians]] arrived in the late 19th century. 

==Etymology==
{{Main|Lost Coast}}
[[File:OregonCoastEcola Edit.jpg|left|thumb|The [[Lost Coast|Lost Coast of Cerdanya]] (''Costa Perduda'') first spotted by the fleet of Bizkarran [[Xenokrates Lizoain]]]]
The name ''Cerdanya'' derives from the Bizkarran ''Karritania'', meaning "land of stone" or "rocky land," from the Bizkarran roots ''[[Bizkarran language|karri]]'' ("stone, rock") and ''-tania'' (place suffix). The territory was first charted by Bizkarran mariners during the early [[Age of Discovery]], who named it for its rugged, mountainous interior visible from the coast.

Under Mesogean influence during the colonial period, the name was adapted to ''Cerretania'', with the initial ''K-'' shifting to ''C-'' following Mesogean phonological conventions. This form was later corrupted under Volisanian and Beron administration: the intervocalic ''-rr-'' evolved to ''-rd-'', and the suffix ''-tania'' underwent palatalization to ''-danya'', yielding the modern form ''Cerdanya''.

The traditional [[Walldungu language|Walldungu]] name for the territory that is now Cerdanya is ''Tinguiririca'', often translated as "round/shining quartz." Recent scholarship suggests it may derive from an older form, possibly ''trintrülica'' or ''tintíuilica'', which can be analyzed as:

* '''trintrü / trintri:''' "curly, wavy, rippled"
* '''lil / lica:''' "cliff, large rock, crag" or "rocky eminence"

The [[Yamanakuta language|Yamanakuta]] name for the territory is ''Lapãyãla'', meaning "rocky land" or "land of crags," from ''lapã'' ("rock, stone, crag") and ''yãla'' ("land, territory"). This parallel with the Bizkarran ''Karritania'' reflects how both indigenous peoples and Bizkarran explorers recognized the territory's defining geological character.

The Sanese pirates led by [[Ryuji Takemoto]] that would [[Cala del Drac|eventually settle]] on the [[Salsipuedes Sea|Salsipuedes]] coast of Cerdanya would name the territory ''Kishimatsu'' ({{Langx|ja|岸松|4=coastal pine}}) in reference to the abundant ''[[W:Pinus|pinus]]'' tree species that they discovered along the coast. It is not clear which species this was in reference to, but it is widely assumed to be either the [[W:Pinus ponderosa|''pinus ponderosa'']] or the [[W:Douglas fir|''pseudotsuga menziesii'']] (Cerdanyan pine).

==History==
===Indigenous peoples===
In the pre-colonial period, Tinguiririca was a land of remarkable diversity, was home to a variety of ecosystems, from the fertile northern plains to the rugged, fjord-filled southern coasts. The Wallche and Yamana tribes were the most prominent indigenous groups in the region. Wallches lived in scattered [[W:Hamlet (place)|hamlets]], mainly along the rivers of [[Zona Nord|''Zona Nord'']]. All major population centres lay at the confluences of rivers. Wallches preferred to build their houses on hilly terrain or isolated hills rather than on plains and [[W:Terrace (geology)|terraces]].
[[File:Araucanos.jpg|left|thumb|323x323px|''Gravadura d'una familha Tinguiriricana'' by [[Guilhèm Arnavielha]] (1783)]]
The [[W:Mapuche|Wallche]] inhabited the northern regions, particularly around present-day [[Cadaqués]] and [[Dríadas]]. They developed advanced farming techniques, including terracing and irrigation, allowing them to sustain large populations, cultivating [[W:Maize|maize]], [[W:Beans|beans]], [[W:Squash|squash]], and [[W:Potatoes|potatoes]], and to a lesser extent practiced hunting and fishing. Early Wallches had two types of leaders, secular and religious. The religious were ''[[W:Machi (shaman)|machi]]'', {{Lang|arn|hechicero}} and the {{Lang|arn|boquivoye}}. The secular were the {{Lang|arn|reche}}, ''[[Ulmen (Mapuche)|ülmen]]'' and {{Lang|arn|gentyoke}}. Later the secular leaders were known as ''[[W:Lonko|lonko]]'', ''[[W:Toqui|toki]]'', {{Lang|arn|ülmen}} and {{Lang|arn|weupin}}. They practiced a [[W:Mapuche religion|polytheistic religion]] with a rich mythology, deeply connected to the natural world.

The politics, economy and religion of the pre- and early-contact Wallches were based on the lineages of local communities called {{Lang|arn|lov}}. This kind of organization was replicated at the larger ''[[W:Rehue|rehue]]'' level that encompassed several {{Lang|arn|lov}}. The politics of each lineage were not equally aggressive or submissive, but different from case to case. Lineages were [[W:Patrilineality|patrilineal]] and [[W:Patrilocal residence|patrilocal]]. [[W:Polygamy|Polygamy]] was common among Wallches and together with the custom of feminine [[W:Exogamy|exogamy]] it has been credited by [[José Bengoa]] with welding the Wallche into one people.

In contrast, the [[W:Yaghan people|Yamana people]] lived a nomadic lifestyle as hunter-gatherers in the southern [[W:Fjord|fjords]] and [[W:Ria|rias]]. They were skilled mariners, using [[W:Dugout canoe|dugout canoes]] and [[W:Dalca|dalcas]]—boats made from planks sewn together with natural fibers—to navigate the complex waterways. The Yamana relied on fishing, hunting sea mammals, and gathering shellfish and wild plants. Their communities were small and highly mobile, adapting to the harsh, variable climate of southern Cerdanya. The Yamana had a profound spiritual connection to the sea and their environment, with intricate rituals and oral traditions passed down through generations.

===Illypno-Fosian arrival and colonization===
{{See also|Reapers' War}}
The beginning of the 16th century marked the beginning of Illypnian contact with Tinguiririca, as Bizkarran explorers, driven by the [[W:Age of discovery|age of discovery]] and chartered by the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Crown of Volisania]], reached its shores. The initial encounters between the Volisanian and the indigenous populations were characterized by mutual curiosity and tentative trade. The Europeans were fascinated by the Wallche's agricultural prowess and the Yamana's maritime skills. The Volisanian expeditions, under the leadership of navigators like [[Álvaro de Almagro]] and [[Tomàs Jaumandreu]], mapped the coastline (paying special attention to the coasts of the [[Sea of Shuainia]]) and established the first tentative [[W:Presidio|fortifications]]. They brought with them Illypnian goods such as metal tools, textiles, and livestock, which they traded with the indigenous peoples in exchange for food, furs, and local knowledge.

Tensions between the Cathars and the Xacano Catholics in the [[Baliscan archipelago]] would eventually escalate into the [[Reapers' War]] (1578-1608). The war's origins lay in the complex interplay of colonial expansion and [[W:Religious intolerance|religious intolerance]] between colonists. The Xacano colonists, seeking to consolidate their control over [[Bayara]] and exploit its resources, viewed the Cathar communities with suspicion. The Cathars, practicing a [[W:Catharism#Suppression|long marginalized]] form of Christianity, were labeled as [[W:Heretics|heretics]] by the [[W:Catholic Church|Catholic Church]], providing a convenient justification for their persecution. The war itself was triggered by a dispute over land rights, but quickly escalated into a broader campaign of [[W:Religious violence|religious violence]]. Catholic militias, often motivated by the promise of land grants and religious zeal, targeted Cathar settlements and [[W:Reductions|reductions]], destroying villages and enslaving many of the indigenous residents they captured. The conflict resulted in the significant migration of Beron and Volisanian-speaking Cathars westward, fleeing the [[W:Religious persecution|religious persecution]] and [[W:Forced expulsion|forced expulsion]] from the Estuarian wetlands. These refugees predominantly settled along the west coast of Tinguiririca, establishing the Colony of Cerdanya. The term ''Rodamóns'' ({{Literal translation|wanderer|vagabond}}) emerged during this period, initially used by the Catholics with a negative, classist connotation to describe the often impoverished Aranese-speaking Cathar refugees subjected to massacres and forced expulsions.

The Cathars, seeking to rebuild their society, brought with them Illypnian agricultural techniques, livestock, and crops. They established settlements that quickly grew into sizable communities, including [[Merced]] and [[Concepción]]. Overall the Wallche of Tinguiririca appear to have been very selective in adopting Illypnian technologies and species, with the exception of horses and wheat. This meant that the Wallche way of living remained largely the same after Volisan contact. The scant adoption of Spanish technology has been characterized as a means of [[W:Cultural resistance|cultural resistance]]. However, their arrival also spread [[W:Old World diseases|Old World diseases]] to which the indigenous populations had no immunity. Epidemics of [[W:Smallpox|smallpox]], [[W:Measles|measles]], and [[W:Influenza|influenza]] swept through Wallche and Yamana communities, causing catastrophic population declines. The Cathars, despite their initial goodwill towards the indigenous peoples, found themselves in a position of increasing dominance. The demographic collapse of the indigenous populations allowed the Cathars to expand their territories and influence more rapidly than they might have otherwise.
[[File:Johann Moritz Rugendas-el rapto.jpg|left|thumb|''El malón'' (1834) by [[W:Johann Moritz Rugendas|Johann Moritz Rugendas]]]]
This colonial expansion southward provoked violent responses from the Wallches, and tribal leaders such as [[Liwen]] resorted to [[W:Malón|''malón'']] (raids) against Illypnian colonists: this consisted of fast surprise attack by a number of mounted Wallche warriors against the white (''[[W:Huinca|huinca]]'') populations, ranches, settlements and fortifications, with the aim of obtaining horses, cattle, provisions, captives, and ultimately forcing the abandonment of the settlements. The rapid attack without formal order did not give the targets time to organize a defense, and it left behind a devastated population unable to retaliate or pursue. 

Despite these raids, various Wallche groups and Cathar authorities met in a diplomatic meeting in 1611, now known as the [[Parliament of Melipulli]] (the fields of Melipulli), agreeing in the ensuing treaty to end their hostilities in order to concentrate their limited resources in resisting Volisan-Catholic expansion. The treaty is often interpreted as an official recognition of the Wallche nation's existence as an sovereign nation. This sovereignty was not limited, but rather established by agreements typical of a conventional alliance between two nations, such as ensuring free transit for certain dignitaries or preventing the free passage of the ally's enemies. In keeping with the diverse nature of the participating cultures, the agreements were not solemnized with a signature, but with gestures during a traditional Wallche [[Elun|e''lun'']] ({{Literal translation|to give}}) ceremony. 

The agreements were: 

* That the Wallches would retain their absolute freedom, without anyone being able to harass them in their territory or hand them over to [[W:Encomenderos|encomenderos]];
* That Wallche territory had [[Hornopirén]] as its northern border;
* That the Wallches should free the Cathar captives they were holding and vice versa;
* Mutual agreement to allow missions that came in peace to engage in trade and enter each others' lands;
* That they agreed to consider the Spanish as enemies and that they would not ally themselves with foreigners who arrived on the coast.

The parliament served as starting point for the return of many of those held captive by the Wallche and Cathars. However not all captives wanted to go back to life among their communities, opting to integrate into the opposing culture. The resulting syncretic culture was unique, blending Cathar, [[Volisania|Volisanian]] and Wallche elements into a distinct proto-Cerdanyan identity.

==== Sanese arrival and settlement ====
{{Further|Great Disappointment|Ryuji Takemoto}}
[[File:Ryuji Takemoto.jpg|thumb|321x321px|An Edo-period [[W:Woodblock printing|woodblock print]] depicting [[Ryuji Takemoto|Takemoto]] with his signature sake cup.]]

The first documented Fosian contact with Tinguiririca occurred not from the established colonial powers of Illypnia but from Sanese refugees fleeing the isolationist policies of the [[Honda Shogunate]]. In late 1724, the disgraced former [[W:Daimyō|daimyō]] [[Ryuji Takemoto]], known in the Shogunate as the "Drunken Dragon of Kazan" and in contemporary Volisanian records as "El Dragón Borracho" or "Takemoto el Pirata," led a flotilla of three ocean-going junks carrying approximately two hundred followers across the [[Shendan Ocean]]. This expedition, comprised of disgraced samurai, disenfranchised peasants, maritime smugglers, and several families including women and children, had embarked on a perilous five-month trans-oceanic voyage fleeing the strict [[W:Sakoku|sakoku laws]] that had exiled Takemoto to the volcanic Kazan Islands following a scandalous disruption at the shogunal court in 1702.[[File:Arrival of Ryuji Takemoto's fleet.png|thumb|Late 18th-century Sanese [[W:Woodblock printing|woodblock print]] depicting the arrival of Ryuji Takemoto's [[W:Junk (ship)|junk fleet]] on the coast of Kishimatsu|left]]The battered fleet made landfall in early 1726 along the rugged, wind-swept southwestern coast of the [[Tinguirirican Peninsula]], in what is now the [[Salsipuedes Sea|Salsipuedes]] region of western Cerdanya. At the time of their arrival, the area remained under nominal Volisanian colonial oversight but was in practice sparsely controlled, with the interior dominated by [[Wallche people|Wallche]] agricultural communities and the [[W:Rias|rias]] and [[W:Fjord|fjord]] coasts inhabited by [[Yamana]] maritime hunter-gatherers. Takemoto's exhausted group established a fortified settlement in a deep, sheltered cove that they named [[Takemoto-no-Sato]], subsequently rendered in Volisanian colonial records as Puerto Takemoto or [[Cala del Drac|''Cala del Drac'']], meaning "Dragon's Cove."

The Sanese settlers called the territory they had discovered ''Kishimatsu'' (岸松, {{Literal translation|coastal pine}}), a name referencing the abundant coniferous forests of the region, believed by scholars to indicate either the [[W:Ponderosa pine|ponderosa pine]] or the [[W:Douglas fir|Cerdanyan pine]] that dominated the coastal landscape. This designation represented one of the earliest non-indigenous toponyms applied to the region and persisted in Sanese-Baliscan community oral tradition long after the abandonment of the original settlement. The toponym ''Kishimatsu'' did not enter official colonial usage, which preferred adaptations of the indigenous Wallche designation Tinguiririca or the [[Bizkarran language|Bizkarran]]-derived ''Cerdanya'', but it remained significant as evidence of the pre-colonial Sanese presence.

Under Takemoto's pragmatic leadership, the Sanese established complex and friendly relationships with the [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous populations of the region]]. Recognizing their precarious position as a small, isolated community without prospect of reinforcement from Fosia, the settlers negotiated tentative but genuinely mutual alliances with local [[Wallche people|Wallche]] and [[Yamana people|Yamana]] groups. They traded high-quality iron tools and weaponry, along with rudimentary agricultural techniques including early capsicum cultivation and [[W:Sake|sake]] distillation methods adapted to local ingredients, in exchange for access to arable land, abundant seafood, and safe harbor in the sheltered cove. These exchanges proved transformative, with Sanese metallurgical techniques and ship repair facilities providing tangible benefits to indigenous communities while the settlers gained crucial knowledge of local navigation, foraging patterns, and survival strategies necessary for enduring the harsh, semi-arid coastal geography punctuated by deep fjords.

{{plain image with caption|Kishimatsu-no-kaizoku banner.jpg|caption=The banner of the [[Kishimatsu-no-kaizoku]] ({{Literal translation|pirates of the coastal pine}}), also known as the ''Náufragos'' ({{Literal translation|shipwrecked|castaways}}) in contemporary [[Baliscano]].|align=right|caption position=bottom|triangle=triangle|triangle color=rgba(0,110,255,1)|upright=2|width=300px}}

The Takemoto settlement remained small and precarious throughout its existence, never exceeding a few hundred residents at its peak. The community survived through a careful combination of localized maritime activity that contemporary colonial authorities classified as piracy, legitimate trade with indigenous partners, and gradual adaptation to the demanding environment. The settlers intermarried with Wallche and Yamana populations, creating a distinct [[Mestizo Baliscans|mestizo community]] that blended Sanese, indigenous, and eventually Illypnian cultural elements. Culinary traditions associated with the settlement, particularly the incorporation of [[W:Capsicum|capsicum]] into preserved foods and the development of early fusion cooking techniques combining Sanese fermentation methods with local ingredients, would later be cited as foundational elements of northwestern Baliscan gastronomy.

The colonial experiment of Kishimatsu proved short-lived. Ryuji Takemoto died around 1729 during a violent skirmish, with historical accounts differing as to whether he fell defending the cove against Volisanian colonial naval patrols attempting to suppress maritime raiding or in conflict with rival indigenous groups contesting control of the coast. Sanese oral tradition maintains that he faced his end with characteristic defiance, sword in one hand and sake cup in the other, embodying the romanticized anti-authoritarian persona that would later elevate him to legendary status in both Cerdanya and [[Okaiken]]. Following his death, the settlement gradually dispersed, with survivors either integrating into indigenous communities, migrating to other coastal areas, or eventually intermarrying with subsequent waves of Illypnian colonists.

The Sanese presence in Kishimatsu, while demographically insignificant in comparison to the subsequent waves of Illypnian colonization, established important precedents for the multicultural character of Cerdanyan settlement. The Takemoto expedition demonstrated that pre-modern trans-oceanic contact between Fosia and Abyala was possible outside the framework of state-sponsored colonial enterprises, and that such settlements could achieve viable, if precarious, coexistence with indigenous populations through mutual exchange rather than conquest. The archaeological remains of Takemoto-no-Sato, including distinctive Sanese ceramic fragments, [[W:Japanese cemeteries and cenotaphs|Sanese cemeteries and cenotaphs]] and other [[Buddhism in Balisca|Buddhist materials]], iron-forging debris, and the foundations of the fortified cove settlement, were identified in the mid-twentieth century and have since been recognized as significant evidence of the pre-modern Sanese diaspora. Contemporary Sanese-Baliscan community organizations trace their heritage partially to these early settlers, and the Kishimatsu narrative has become incorporated into the broader mythology of Balisca's multicultural foundations, representing an eighteenth-century bridge between Fosia and the Abyalan continent that predates both the formal colonial projects of Illypnia and the later waves of official contract labor migration that would significantly expand the Sanese-Baliscan population following the 1808 independence.


===''Volisanya Boba'' and confederation===
{{Main|Volisanya Boba|Continuation War|Baliscan Civil War}}

{| width="20%" align="right"
| align="center" |[[File:Flag of the Commonwealth of Cerdanya.svg|border|150x150px|England]]
| align="center" |[[File:Flag of the Northern Confederation.svg|150x150px|Aragon]]
|-
|}
The Cerdanyan colonies were amongst the most decentralized of the Illypnian colonies on Abyala, owing to their repeated history of displacement stretching all the way back to the [[Andalusian Crusade]] (1209-1229).
[[File:Nova_planta_Catalunya_1.jpg|right|thumb|360x360px|Cover of the [[Nueva Planta decrees]] of the [[W:Principality of Catalonia|Principality of Catalonia]]]]
Angered by what he saw as [[W:Sedition|sedition]] by the [[Crown of Aragon|realms of the Crown of Aragon]] (including Cerdanya and much of the Baliscan archipelago) which had supported the claim of [[W:Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles of Austria]] to the Spanish thrones during the [[W:War of Spanish Succession|War of Spanish Succession]] and taking his native [[W:Kingdom of France|France]] as a model of a centralized state, Philip V [[W:Nueva Planta decrees|suppressed the institutions]], privileges, and the ancient [[W:Fuero|charters]] of almost all the areas that were formerly part of the Crown of Aragon ([[W:Kingdom of Aragon|Kingdom of Aragon]], [[W:Principality of Catalonia|Principality of Catalonia]], [[W:Kingdom of Valencia|Kingdom of Valencia]], and the [[W:Kingdom of Majorca|Kingdom of Majorca]]). The decrees ruled that all the territories in the Crown of Aragon except the [[W:Aran Valley|Aran Valley]] were to be ruled by the laws of [[W:Crown of Castile|Castile]] ("the most praiseworthy in all the Universe" according to the 1707 decree), embedding those regions into a new and nearly uniformly administered, centralised [[W:Spain|Spain]]. 
[[File:Revolt_of_the_Comuneros.jpg|left|thumb|The [[Revolt of the Comuneros|1712 peasant revolt]] in the city of [[Merced]] against Philip V. The painting depicts the ''comuneros'' taking up the image of the Holy Christ as the banner of the uprising, in [[Rodrigo de la Sierra]]'s 1908 portrait, ''[[Revolta dels Comuneros (painting)|Revolta dels Comuneros]]'']]
This event greatly impacted the governance and threatened the relative autonomy that the colonies had under the jurisdiction of the Crown of Aragon. Additionally, the population, at this time composed mainly of [[Catharism|Cathars]], [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic Jews]], [[W:Muladí|Muslims]], [[W:Mozarabs|Mozarabs]], and [[W:Mudéjar|Mudéjars]], staunchly opposed the centralization of power imposed by the Nueva Planta decrees. Having experienced violent persecution against their respective communities, they adamantly rejected the authority of the now-dominant Castilians, and simmering unrest led to the [[Revolt of the Comuneros]] in the Cerdanyan provinces and the formation of the [[Junta of Merced]]. The Junta was quickly endorsed by popular consensus, leading to the establishment of the [[Commonwealth of Cerdanya]].

[[W:Spain under Joseph Bonaparte|Spain under Joseph Bonaparte]] attempted to forcibly re-impose colonial rule in the Baliscan archipelago, marking the end of the [[Espanya Boba|"Espanya Boba"]] period of Baliscan history stretching back to the early 18th century. Although there has been research on the idea of a pan-Baliscan identity that overlapped the early political boundaries, political unification of the archipelago was not the aim of most, nor was it necessarily inevitable. In fact, there was little coordination between the different factions in the opening years, despite their mutual interest in resisting the Spanish attempt at conquest.

Unlike the other [[W:Spanish American Wars of Independence|Spanish American Wars of Independence]], the ''Resistencía'' was primarily fought to preserve the political independence of the  against an attempted imperial ''reconquista'', as opposed to the civil wars between colonists seen in South America.

In 1815, representatives of [[Bayara|Royalist Bayara]] surrendered to the confederal forces following the decisive [[Battle of Navassa]], effectively destroying the last royalist strongholds in mainland Balisca, allowing the rebels to consolidate, ending the threat of invasion against the Confederation. While this did not change the position of Spain against separatism, it resulted in the collapse of royalists forces in Balisca. Over the course of the next two years, the Anti-royalist armies won major victories and consolidated their control in their respective provinces. The continued political instability in Spain, without a navy, army or treasury, convinced many former royalists of the need to formally abandon their cause and embrace independence.

=== Civil war and proto-industrialization ===
{{Main|Confederal Question|Baliscan Civil War|Zuau}}

[[File:Zouave_-_Grolleron_crop.jpg|left|thumb|298x298px|Cerdanyan ''[[Zuau]]'' in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], {{circa|1825}}]]
Immediately following the [[Treaty of Navassa|securing of independence]], dissatisfaction with the confederal system of governance emerged. The republics began to look to their own interests rather than the confederation. During the war, confederal forces had imprisoned, hanged, or expelled all former slave owners (most relocated to [[W:Empire of Brazil|Brazil]] and Argentina), and divided their land and other assets between the newly emancipated African population. This further hardened attitudes in Bayara against confederation, which already was strong due to the devastation in Bayara in terms of infrastructure and economy. Because of the reluctance of the land-owning class accept the loss of their privileges and to grant voting rights to freedmen, the Confederal Congress adopted [[Reconstruction laws]] over Bayaran objections. In response, and intending to avoid the social effects of the war, most of the Bayaran provinces passed [[W:Black codes|black codes]]. These were mostly ''de jure'' nullified by confederal law and pro-reformist legislatures, but widely continued to be enforced by Bayaran officials. Increasing calls for the strengthening of confederal power and increasing Hesperian influence in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]] fueled inter-communal tensions between predominately Catalan-speaking [[Catharism|Cathars]] and Aragonese-speaking Catholics, and the confederal government was increasingly dragged into the position of mediator in the region. Conflict boiled over following the murder of an Catalan [[W:Pastry|pastry]] chef in [[Xàbia]] by a Catholic mob, resulting in [[Pastry War|escalating reprisals and counter-reprisals]] which eventually culminated in [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]]. It was ultimately ended by the [[Treaty of Navassa]].

The treaty led to the ''de facto'' partition of Bayara on [[W:Ethnoreligious group|ethnoreligious]] lines, which was propped up by the intervention of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] (popularly known as ''zuau'') to administer the Estuarian territories, which largely prevented further violence. Bayaran dissatisfaction with the terms of the Treaty led to it's delegates blocking the allocation of confederal funding for the Legion in the [[Confederal Congress]], ultimately forcing its disbandment in the spring of 1826.

Hesperian delegates called for a [[Confederal Convention]] to solve the impending crisis, to be held in [[Alessandria]]. The convention resulted in the [[Compromise of 1827]] which provided for a significantly strengthened central government to handle disputes between the respective republics and their citizens. This was unanimously supported by the delegations of Hesperia and Cerdanya, but was rejected and ultimately ignored by the Bayarans, with the exception of the delegation from [[Macaio]]. These delegates were at the time aware that the republic was well on it's way towards renouncing the [[Articles of Confederation]] and {{W|secession|seceding}} from the Confederation, and sought to pre-empt this by declaring the [[Carpinchera Republic]]. The Junta announced it's denunciation of Articles of Confederation on 6 July 1828, and the secessionist [[Xunta Nacionalista]] assumed provisional powers in [[Baliscan Civil War|anticipation of war]].

Despite controlling a numerical troop advantage throughout the war, the Bayarans were unable to overcome its geographic disadvantages: it was relatively flat, it was unable to establish control over most high mountain passes across the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], and thus were unable to directly threaten [[Hesperia|Hesperian territory]]. Notably however, Bayaran armies crossed the [[Cerroitá Pass]] and attempted to capture the [[Alpujarra]], culminating in the [[Siege of Castejón]], which was beaten back following the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] in support of confederal forces. Bayara suffered substantially more than the rest of the Confederation overall, as the confederal strategy of [[W:Attrition warfare|attrition warfare]] meant that Bayaran forces could not replace their casualties, and the total war waged by Hesperian armies devastated the infrastructure and caused widespread poverty and distress.

By 1835, most Bayaran pro-secessionist land and naval forces had either disbanded, surrendered or otherwise ceased hostilities. Bayara was punished severely in the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]]: they were forced to pay the expenses and debts incurred by the confederation during the war, in addition to the General Junta being coerced into ratifying the Compromise, resulting in the end of the Confederal Question, and marking the beginning of the Reconstruction era'''.'''

==== Reconstruction and rapid industrialization ====
{{Main|Anyos Federales|Tinguiririca conflict}}

Domestically, the economy of the Cerdanyan provinces were overwhelmingly [[W:Agrarian society|agrarian]]; however, the agricultural surplus produced what historians call a "pre-industrial" transformation in Northwestern Cerdanya starting from the late 1820s. However the economy prospered during the years of the civil war, in part due to the fielding of a large confederal army and navy which heavily relied on raw materials from Cerdanya. This led to a diffuse, if mostly artisanal, concentration of manufacturing activities, especially under the economically liberal policies adopted by the emerging federal administration in the aftermath of the civil war. The [[Industrial Revolution in Balisca|Baliscan Industrial Revolution]] was launched along the [[Strait of Ivorra]] in 1840, where [[W:Textile mills|textile mills]] spread across [[Merced]], and in southern [[Cadaqués]], where coal, steel, and industrialization launched the republic's first experiments in the manufacturing sector.

In 1853 gold was discovered in southern Cerdanya (present day [[Muntanya]] and [[Provence]]), sparking the [[Ivorran Gold Rush]]. The allure of potentially striking gold and achieving wealth brought approximately 300,000 people to Cerdanya, both from the rest of the confederation and from abroad, many of them Europeans, Arabs, Chinese and smaller numbers of Africans. The influx of gold into the money supply reinvigorated the struggling Baliscan economy; and the rapid population increase allowed for Cerdanya to rapidly accumulate [[Cataloccitan aristocracy|outsized economic and political power]] in the confederation. However, the gold rush had severe effects on the Indigenous people of Tinguiririca, accelerating their population's decline from disease, starvation, intensifying the [[Tinguiririca conflict]] with increased settler-colonial encroachment on their territories. 
[[File:Claude Monet, The Gare St-Lazare, 1877.jpg|thumb|Claude Monet, ''Estació de Sant Jordi, la tren de Ivorra,'' 1876]]
With the birth of the [[Anyos Federales|federal state]], there was a deep consciousness in the [[Cataloccitan aristocracy|emerging ruling class]] of the new country's backwardness, given that the per capita GDP expressed in PPS terms was roughly half of that of Britain and about 25% less than that of France and Germany. During the 1860s and 1870s, the manufacturing activity was relatively artisan and small-scale, while the oversized agrarian sector was the backbone of the republic's economy. The country however had large coal and iron deposits, which allowed a rapid expansion in [[W:Steam power|steam power]] and advances in iron and [[W:Steel production|steelmaking]]. Conflicts with Indigenous peoples regarding territorial exploration and settlement were arbitrated by the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. This prompted the introduction of more modern farming techniques in the rural interior, while from 1878 to 1887 protectionist policies were introduced with the aim to establish a heavy industry base. Large steel and iron works soon clustered around areas of high hydropower potential, notably the foothills of the [[Southern Pyrenees]], and Merced and Concepción captured the textile, chemicals, engineering and banking boom.

However, the diffusion of industrialisation that characterized the northern area of the republic largely excluded the southern extremities of the island of Tinguiririca, which [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|remained predominately inhabited]] by indigenous peoples. 

=== ''Cerdañazo'' and political dissolution ===
{{Main|Baliscan Revolution|Tragic Week}}

Prosperity stemming from the reforms adopted during the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] led to the development of [[Health care in Balisca|universal health care]] (1914), [[Baliscan Pension Plan|old-age pensions]] (1915), and [[Veterans Affairs (Balisca)|veterans' pensions]] (1915). In addition, universal free education allowed for sizable increases in educational enrollment and development, giving rise to a new generation of social development across the Confederation. The economy grew 12% annually, surpassing that of the rest of the [[W:ABC countries|ABC countries]] combined. 

However, this rapid export driven growth of Balisca's economy was eclipsed by the increasing stratification of Baliscan society, largely through the domination of the [[Cataloccitan aristocracy]] based in Cerdanya and the economic disruption caused by the [[W:First World War|First World War]]. The victory of known [[W:Crony capitalism|crony capitalist]] "[[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|Progressive]]" [[Pol Pérez]] in the [[1918 Cerdanyan election]] was widely condemned as fraudulent by vast swathes of the Cerdanyan electorate. Left-wing activists demanded the return toppling of the aristocracy and an election re-run, spreading from Cerdanya across the nation, urging unions to stage marches and protests across the nation. Over four million answered their calls on 28 October 1918, in what would become known as the [[1917 Baliscan general strike|1918 Baliscan general strike]], in which millions of students, laborers, and unions took to the streets of major Baliscan cities, bring them to a halt.
[[File:Milano,_Galleria_Vittorio_Emanuele_II_(bombardata)_01.jpg|left|thumb|276x276px|The destroyed interior of the ''[[Galleria Mercedesa]]'' following [[Tragic Week (Balisca)|targeted bombings]] on the night of 26 April]]
The movement rapidly gained confederal attention, and garnered wide support after the assassination of several leading figures of the movement was exposed as tied to Pérez's [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|Progress Party]]. Immediately the government stepped up efforts to have the movement suppressed, causing a spike in support for it. Protests grew rapidly, with Concepción being their focal point. An anti-government gathering at [[Cooperative University of Concepción|Universitat Cooperativa de Concepción]] was attacked by the Civil Guard, resulting in 52 people killed. The protesters, comprised of liberals, libertarians, feminists, socialists, and anarchist quickly spread throughout the country, while the crisis broke international headlines.
[[File:Flag of Cerdanya (1919).svg|thumb|Flag of the [[Cerdanyan Soviet Republic]]]]
The movement escalated on 21 April 1919, with the [[storming of the Baliscan Cabildo]] by rebelling soldiers supported by civilians in reaction to increasingly violent repression by the state. The successful coup against the Confederal Congress and the seizure the Cabildo [[W:Power of the deed|instigated]] the second, more violent phase of the Baliscan Revolution, ultimately leading to a significant rise in {{W|political violence}} nationwide. Tensions further escalated with the assassination of Deputy Premier [[Moisès Vilchez]] by [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] [[Miquel Cebrián]]. With the country on the verge of a violent {{W|civil war}}, the Council of Ministers narrowly voted against {{W|vote of no confidence|confidence}} in Premier [[Máximo Burjassot]], forcing the administration to dissolve. Faced with continued unrest on the streets and an increasingly demoralized and hostile military, the Confederal Council called a [[1919 Confederal Convention|Confederal Convention]]. 

The revolution resulted in the abolition of the Baliscan republican system and the widespread implementation of [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] and more specifically [[W:libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] principles throughout various portions of society, including the {{W|free migration|abolition of internal borders}}, {{W|secularization}}, and {{W|democratic socialism}}. Much of the [[economy of Balisca]] was put under {{W|worker control}}, while {{W|social welfare}} was significantly expanded through the {{W|redistribution of wealth|redistribution of income and wealth}}. Worker committees gained control of their factories, [[W:agriculture|agrarian]] areas became collectivized and run as libertarian socialist {{W|agricultural commune}}s.

The revolution had far reaching effects in Cerdanya's economic structure with the expanded power of workers and their unions, ultimately leading to the development of Balisca's current form of {{W|market socialism}}. Socioeconomic policy was also heavily influenced by the [[W:Futurism|futurist]] movements that swept Balisca after the revolution, eventually coalescing into [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] ideology. In addition, the Revolution hastened the process of {{W|indigenous decolonization}} which had begun with the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. Additionally, the Revolution has significant international repercussions, especially regarding Baliscan relations with emerging leftist movements across the world. The revolution served as the precursor to similar social movements in Latin America and other developing regions.

==Geography and climate==
{{Main|Geography of Cerdanya}}

[[File:Cadaqués coast.png|thumb|275x275px|[[Sehaska]], meaning "cradle", is a coastal area in [[Cadaqués]]. It is made up of rugged [[W:fjord|fjords]] and [[W:Ria|rias]]]]Located at the northern edge of the [[Abyala|Abyalan]] continent, Cerdanya is the northernmost and westernmost territory in Balisca and the [[Northern Triangle]]. The {{convert|10|km}} wide [[Sea of Shuainia]] (considered a part of the wider [[Northern Ocean]]) separates Cerdanya from [[Arktici]]. Cerdanya's territorial waters touch (and by Cerdanya's claims, include) that of the {{W|Drake Passage|Mar de Hoces}} and {{W|Antarctica}}, which under international opinion, is suspended. Cerdanya has a longer coastline than all the other communities within Balisca.
[[File:Sonoro Balisca.png|left|thumb|[[Rukapillañ]] is the highest mountain in Balisca, and the second-highest in the {{W|Southern Cone}}, at {{convert|4822.6|m}}|285x285px]]
At {{convert|1736790|km2|0}} in area [{{convert|259090|km2|0}} not including [[Baliscan Antarctica]]], Cerdanya is by far the largest community in the Baliscan Confederation, and is more than twice the size of the second-largest community, [[Bayara]]. Cerdanya is the seventh [[w:List of the largest country subdivisions by area|largest subnational division in the world]], and if it was an independent nation would be the 16th largest country in the world, as it is larger than [[TBA]].

The [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] and the [[Máfil Bay]]'s many inlets provide some of Balisca's most renowned and spectacular scenery, which forms the backdrop and context for a growing outdoor adventure and ecotourism industry. 75 percent of the community is mountainous (more than 1,000 metres (3,300 ft) above sea level); 60 percent is forested; and only about 5 percent is arable.

The interior of Tinguiririca away from the coastal regions is somewhat moderated by the [[Northern Ocean]]. Terrain ranges from dry inland forests and semi-arid valleys, to the range and canyon districts of the Central Interior, to boreal forest and subarctic prairie in the Northern Interior. High mountain regions both north and east have {{W|subalpine flora}} and {{W|subalpine climate}}.
[[File:Kuyenmapu glacier.png|thumb|275x275px|[[Quilicura Glacier]] in [[Küyenmapu]]]]
With its myriad islands, Cerdanya has nearly {{convert|34000|mi|km|-3}} of tidal shoreline. The Carlemapu Islands chain extends west from the southern tip of the [[Bajo Nevada Peninsula]]. Many active {{W|volcano}}es are found in [[Küyenmapu]] and in coastal regions. [[Futrono Island]], for example, is home to [[Lácar]], which is an occasionally smoldering volcano that rises to {{convert|10000|ft|m|-3}} above the land. The chain of volcanoes extends to all the way to [[Pico Ardiente]], west of [[Sueñodorado]] on the island of [[Piscaria]]. Cerdanya has more than one million lakes. {{W|Marshland}}s and wetland {{W|permafrost}} cover {{convert|188320|sqmi|km2|}} (mostly in far south flatlands). Glacier ice covers about {{convert|28957|sqmi|km2}} of Cerdanya. The [[Butacura Glacier]] is the largest glacier in Balisca, covering {{convert|2008|sqmi|km2}} alone.

Northwestern Cerdanya has a subpolar {{W|oceanic climate}} ([[W:Köppen climate classification|Köppen climate classification]] ''Cfc'') with short, cool summers and long, wet, moderately mild winters: the precipitation averages {{convert|3000|mm|in|0|abbr=on}} a year in the far west, but precipitation decreases rapidly to the eastern side. Temperatures are steady throughout the year: in Puerto Baliscano they hardly surpass {{convert|9|°C|°F|sigfig=2}} in summers and average {{convert|0|°C|°F|sigfig=2}} in winters. Snowfall can occur in summer. The cold and wet summers help preserve the ancient {{W|glacier}}s. The southernmost islands possess a sub-antarctic climate typical of tundra that makes the growth of trees impossible. Some areas in the interior have a polar climate. Regions in the world with similar climates to southern Cerdanya are: the {{W|Aleutian islands}}, {{W|Iceland}}, the {{W|Alaska Peninsula}}, the {{W|Faroe Islands}}, {{W|Tierra del Fuego}}, and the {{W|Heard and McDonald Islands}}.
[[File:Huemul - IMG 8355 - Flickr - v.requesens.jpg|thumb|267x267px|The [[W:South Andean deer|Cerdanyan deer]], known as the ''güemul'' or ''huemul''|left]]

=== Fauna ===
The [[W:Guanaco|guanaco]] (''Lama guanicoe''), [[W:South American cougar|Abyalan cougar]] (''Puma concolor concolor''), the [[W:South American gray fox|Cerdanyan fox]] (''Lycalopex griseus''), [[W:Humboldt's hog-nosed skunk|Cerdanyan hog-nosed skunk]] (''Conepatus humboldtii''), and [[W:Magellanic tuco-tuco|Magellanic tuco-tuco]] (''Ctenomys magellanicus''; a subterranean [[W:Rodent|rodent]]) are the most characteristic mammals of the Cerdanyan plains. The [[Cerdanyan steppe]] is one of the last strongholds of the guanaco and [[W:Darwin's rhea|Darwin's rheas]] (''Rhea pennata''), which had been hunted for their skins by the [[W:Tehuelches|Tehuelches]], on foot using [[W:Boleadoras|boleadoras]], before the diffusion of [[W:Firearms|firearms]] and [[W:Horses|horses]]; they were formerly the chief means of subsistence for the natives, who hunted them on horseback with dogs and [[W:Bolas|bolas]]. [[W:Vizcacha|Vizcachas]] (''Lagidum'' spp.) and the [[W:Patagonian mara|Cerdanyan mara]] (''Dolichotis patagonum'') are also characteristic of the steppe and the pampas to the north. 
[[File:Tinguirirican bear.png|thumb|The [[Tinguirirican bear]] (''Arctotherium'' ''tinguiriricana''), also known to the indigenous [[W:Mapuche people|Wallche]] as the ''Newen'' ("spirit bearer")]]
Bird life is very abundant. The [[W:Crested caracara|crested caracara]] (''Caracara plancus'') is one of the characteristic aspects of a Cerdanyan landscape; the presence of [[W:Austral parakeet|austral parakeets]] (''Enicognathus ferrugineus'') as far south as the shores of the strait attracted the attention of the earlier navigators, and [[W:Green-backed firecrown|green-backed firecrowns]] (''Sephanoides sephaniodes''), a species of [[W:Hummingbird|hummingbird]], may be seen flying amid the snowfall. Of the many kinds of waterfowl the [[W:Chilean flamingo|Cerdanyan flamingo]] (''Phoenicopterus cerdanya''), the [[W:Upland goose|upland goose]] (''Chloephaga picta''), and in the strait, the remarkable [[W:Steamer duck|steamer ducks]] are found.

Signature marine fauna include the [[W:Southern right whale|southern right whale]], the [[W:Magellanic penguin|Magellanic penguin]] (''Spheniscus magellanicus''), the [[W:Killer whale|killer whale]], and [[W:Elephant seal|elephant seals]]. The [[Bajo Nevada Peninsula]] is a AESCO [[W:World Heritage Site|World Heritage Site]], designated for its global significance as a site for the conservation of [[W:Marine mammals|marine mammals]].

The Cerdanyan freshwater fish fauna is relatively restricted compared to other similar Northern Hemisphere regions. The northern region is home to a total of 29 freshwater fish species, 18 of which are native. The [[W:Introduced species|introduced]] are several species of [[W:Trout|trout]], [[W:Common carp|common carp]], and various species that originated in more southern parts of Abyala. The natives are [[W:Osmeriformes|osmeriforms]] (''[[W:Aplochiton|Aplochiton]]'' and ''[[W:Galaxias|Galaxias]]''), [[W:Temperate perch|temperate perches]] (''[[W:Percichthys|Percichthys]]''), [[W:Catfish|catfish]] (''[[W:Diplomystes|Diplomystes]]'', ''[[W:Hatcheria|Hatcheria]]'' and ''[[W:Trichomycterus|Trichomycterus]]''), [[W:Neotropical silverside|Neotropical silversides]] (''[[W:Odontesthes|Odontesthes]]'') and [[W:Characiform|characiforms]] (''[[W:Astyanax (fish)|Astyanax]]'', ''[[W:Cheirodon|Cheirodon]]'', ''[[W:Gymnocharacinus|Gymnocharacinus]]'', and ''[[W:Oligosarcus|Oligosarcus]]''). Other Cerdanyan freshwater fauna include the highly unusual [[W:Aeglidae|aeglid]] crustaceans.

