[[File:Portopi Merced.png|left|thumb|300x300px|Merced's [[Portopí, Merced|Portopí]] barrio is the largest {{W|mixed use development}} project in Cerdanya]]
Cerdanya maintains an {{W|advanced economy|advanced}} {{W|market socialism|market socialist}} system based on the [[W:Doughnut economics|doughnut economic model]]. As of the first quarter of 2022, Cerdanya's gross domestic product was estimated at US$1.188 trillion, ranking it as the third largest economy in the Baliscan Confederation, and the fourth largest in [[Abyala]]. Cerdanya is responsible for around 1/6th of Balisca’s economic activity. The economy of Cerdanya strongly benefits from high rates of worker participation, a highly educated population, low levels of corruption, and its well developed transportation and {{W|telecommunication}} networks.  

In 2019, Cerdanya alone was responsible for around 30% of Balisca’s total trading activity. As a mainstay to the economy, the total value of trade conducted in Cerdanya was estimated to be well over ##% of the country’s GDP, making it one of the most trade dependent countries in the world. The [[Port of Concepción]] is one of the largest in Latin America, and one of the largest and busiest in the world, handling a record ### million tons of cargo in 2019. 

As of 2023, Cerdanya overall has an average poverty rate of 0.9%, one of lowest rates in the confederation. Many coastal and formerly gold-mining communities (particularly in the North) include some of the most [[Social Development Index|socially developed]] areas in the Baliscan Confederation. While progress has been made, there are sizeable income disparities between urban population centers and rural/isolated indigenous communities remain, and worsened during the {{W|Great Recession}}. Adversely, Cerdanya has a moderate level of income inequality, and in 2022 earned a GINI score of 21.5 (compared to the Baliscan average of 20.1).
[[File:Pirihueico canal south.png|thumb|250x250px|A [[Tricontinental Company]] vessel traversing the [[Pirihueico Canal]]. The canal significantly cuts shipping times between {{W|South America}} and the rest of the {{W|South Atlantic Ocean}}]]
{{W|Copper}}, {{W|silver}}, {{W|aluminum}} and {{W|gold}} exports played an important role in the development of Cerdanya's economy post-confederation, especially in the central and southern regions of Cerdanya. More than 85% of Cerdanya's economy was based on the exports of [[W:Natural resources|natural resources]] in 1900. Cerdanya has since then reduced its dependency on mineral exports, by focusing on the expansion of tourism, finance, and construction sectors. Cerdanya alone was responsible for 60% of Balisca's [[W:Mining|mining]] output in 2018. As a mainstay to the economy, mining directly contributes around 20% of Cerdanya's total gross domestic product and 39% of the republic's exports. In addition to being an important supplier of energy, Cerdanya is now becoming an increasingly relevant consumer of energy. The region has established a long tradition of responsible energy stewardship as it develops and diversifies its economy, accelerating the development of new [[W:Renewable energy|renewable energy]] sources.

As of January 2022, Cerdanya's [[W:Public debt|public debt]] was more around $151 billion, or 15.37 percent of its annual [[W:Gross domestic product|gross domestic product]], the third largest of all communities in Balisca. The large budget deficits and government debt since the 2008 global recession and followed by decreases in mining profits has caused concern for the future of the industry. Meanwhile, the {{W|service sector}} accounts for three quarters of the GDP. Cerdanya historically had a large industrial capacity prior to [[W:Deindustrialization|deindustrialization]] which began in the 1970s, and is home to some of the largest and most technologically advanced producers of [[W:Rolling stock|rolling stock]], electronics, machine tools, steel and nonferrous metals, ships, chemical substances, textiles, and processed foods.

Agricultural businesses in Cerdanya cultivate around 10 percent of Cerdanyan land, mostly in [[Provence]] and [[Pigüe]]. Cerdanyan regions account for nearly 25 percent of the annual Baliscan fish catch, second only to that of [[Hesperia]]. As of 2020, Cerdanya's labor force consisted of some 19.9 million workers. Cerdanya has a low unemployment rate of around four percent. Housing in Cerdanya is characterized by limited land supply in urban areas.
===Energy, infrastructure, and environment===
{{Main|Energy in Balisca|Transport in Balisca|Environment of Balisca|AVB}}

[[File:Gombren dam and PV farm.png.jpg|left|thumb|275x275px|The [[Gombrén Photovoltaic Dam]], built near the city of [[Silico]], is one of the world's first combined photovoltaic hydroelectric power stations]]
In the last three decades, Cerdanya has led Balisca in {{W|renewable energy commercialization}}, and has become one of the world's largest producers of {{W|renewable energy}}, ranking as the largest producer in Balisca and the #-largest producer in the world; [[W:Wind power|wind power]] and [[W:Hydroelectricity|hydroelectricity]] are the two largest sources of electricity. Renewable sources account for 63.5% of all electricity produced in Cerdanya, with wind alone reaching 38.6%, followed by hydro at 11.2%, solar at 10.1%, bioenergy at 3.5%, and geothermal at 1.6%. The rest of the community's demand is covered (30%) by {{W|thorium-based nuclear power}} plants, which are a relatively new development in the community.

In 2021, the transport sector in Cerdanya generated a turnover of about 173 billion sol, employing 1.2 million persons in 98,700 enterprises. Regarding the road network, in 2022 there were {{convert|368721|km}} of serviceable roads in Cerdanya, including {{convert|2811|km}} of {{W|motorways}}, state-owned but [[W:Cooperative|cooperatively]] operated by ''[[Atlantia]]''. In 2021, about 1,626,587 passenger cars (590 cars per 1,000 people) and 782,732 goods vehicles circulated on the national road network.
[[File:Aerial_view_of_Hokuto_Chuo_Interchange_and_its_surrounding_area_-_Nov_9,_2024.jpg|thumb|300x300px|[[Sumacàrcer]] and its surroundings looking from the north. The white line stretching straight from the upper left to the lower right is the [[Concepción–Merced high-speed rail line|HSR ''Mercepción'']]. The curved line below it is the [[Autopista of Balisca|Autopista 12]].]]
Alongisde [[Hesperia]], Cerdanya pioneered the construction of some of the first Baliscan motorways, as part of the the so-called ''[[Autopista of Balisca|autopista]]'', reserved for fast traffic and for motor vehicles only. The crossing of the [[Strait of Ivorra]] is an important route for Baliscan transportation, as the strait is a significant natural barrier between the community and [[Atlántida]]. Railway routes crossing the strait began operations in 1978 with the [[Trans-Ivorra Railroad]], followed in 1993 by the [[tba Tunnel]] and the [[tba Tunnel]] in 2007. Cerdanya has the least vehicles per capita, with 158 per 1000 people in 2022. The community's inland waterways network has a length of {{convert|1000|km}} for commercial traffic in 2022.

The national railway network, which is fully [[Baliscan Railways Group#Structure|cooperatively-owned and operated]], in 2021 totaled {{convert|20147|km}} of which {{convert|9879|km}} electrified, and on which 5,314 locomotives and railcars run. The public operator of rail (including high-speed) trains in Cerdanya is ''[[Transcerdaña]]'', part of the [[Baliscan Railways Group]]. Cerdanya has eleven rail crossings over the Southern Pyrenees connecting it's two coasts.
==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Balisca}}

The [[2025 Baliscan census]] enumerated a total population of 30,544,142, an increase of around 6.2 percent over the 2020 figure, and Cerdanya became the second community to cross a population of 30 million (after [[Bayara]] in 2023). Since the early 2010s, the main driver of population growth in Cerdanya has been [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration]], with a steady decline in natural births since the beginning of the 21st century. Cerdanya has one of the highest per-capita immigration rates in the world, driven mainly by economic policy and also [[W:Family reunification|family reunification]]. A record 405,000 immigrants were admitted to Cerdanya in 2023. Balisca leads the world in refugee resettlement; and Cerdanya resettled more than 28,000 in 2018.

Cerdanya spans latitudinally from the [[W:40th parallel south|45th parallel south]] to the [[W:60th parallel south|60th parallel south]] and approximately 95 percent of the population is found north of the [[W:45th parallel south|45th parallel south]]. About 80 percent of the population lives within the [[Gran País del Sur]] ecoregion. The community is highly urbanized, with over 90 percent of the population living in urban centers. The most densely populated part of the country, accounting for nearly 50 percent, is the ''[[Mercepción]]'' corridor in Northern Cerdanya, along the [[Strait of Ivorra]].

The majority of Cerdanyans (81.1 percent) live in family households, 12.1 percent report living alone, and those living with other relatives or unrelated persons reported at 6.8 percent. Fifty-eight percent of households are couples with or without children, 8.7 percent are single-parent households, 12.9 percent are multigenerational households, and 7.7 percent are [[W:Polyamory|polyamorous]] households, and 12.7 percent are single-person households.

