====Immigration====
{{Main|Immigration to Balisca}}

[[File:Rocalish immigrants in Concepcion.jpg|thumb|Painting of [[Skadian Baliscans|Skadian]] immigrants disembarking a [[Tricontinental Company]] Transa-Ethiopian [[W:Ocean liner|ocean liner]] in [[Concepción]]]]
According to the 2020 census data, there are 6,256,210 foreign residents in Cerdanya, comprising 22.6% of the community's population. When including those with a one parent of another nationality, the number rises to 8,664,574 (31.3%) of all Cerdanyans having full or partial roots outside of the country. In the 21st century, emerging communities across Cerdanya include Breislanders, [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzans]], [[Jade Baliscans|Jade]], [[Ayorin|Ayori]], [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayanis]], and [[Kemali Baliscans|Kemalis]]. The increasing number of immigrants has resulted in an extended period of high population growth, despite Cerdanya's birth rate being well below the [[W:Replacement level|replacement level]]. Since the 21st century, immigration has been predominately by air, which has contributed to increasing numbers of [[W:Statelessness|stateless persons]] and the phenomenon of unsheltered people seeking shelter or refuge in [[W:Points of entry|points of entry]] (such as airports and ports).

After the securing of independence in 1818, a lack of economic attractions and successive [[Baliscan Civil War|civil wars]] provided few incentives for immigration to the region, with the exception of German immigration to northern Cerdanya, who were contracted by the Cerdanyan government and played a substantial role in the development of the [[W:Logging industry|logging industry]]. Breislandic immigration included the founding of [[Las Alemáns]] in 1843, and German traders played a significant economic role particularly on the [[Sea of Shuainia]]. 

Most of the immigrants to Cerdanya during the 19th and 20th centuries came from abroad, especially following the discovery of gold in 1853, and the subsequent [[Cerdanyan gold rush]]. Settlers from Illypnia came from [[Volisania]], [[Hersatia]], [[Andalus]], [[Breisland]], and [[Sarta]], among others.  

Cerdanya, like the rest of Balisca remains a major destination for immigrants, accepting on average 400,000 immigrants annually. It is also the leading recipient of asylum seekers in Balisca, with an estimated 45,000 applicants in 2018. In 2023, 233,784 immigrants gained citizenship, amongst whom the majority originated from [[Dagit]], the [[Kemal]], and [[Daras]]. There is a very recent inflow of Abyalans: [[Potocsí|Potocsinos]], [[tba]], [[tba]], [[tba]]. University in Cerdanya, [[Education in Balisca|like the rest of Balisca]], is free, and many international students come to study at Cerdanyan universities. Many people from neighboring [[Asikyira]] and [[Lacashe]], who frequently travel to Cerdanya to spend their holidays, have chosen it as permanent residence. In a very recent trend, Southern Illypnians are also choosing to retire in Cerdanya, which can be attributed to similar climates.

