===Religion===
{{Main|Religion in Balisca}}

{{See also|Irreligion in Balisca}}{{Pie chart|thumb=right|caption=Religion in Cerdanya (2025)|label2=[[Catharism]]|value2=22.8|color2=DarkOrchid|label3={{W|Islam}} (incl. {{W|Sunni}}, {{W|Shia}} and {{W|Liberalism and progressivism within Islam|Progressive}})|value3=8.9|color3=DarkGReen|label1={{W|Irreligious}}|value1=62.6|color1=DodgerBlue|label4={{W|Christianity}} (incl. {{W|Catholicism}}, [[w:Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern]] and [[w:Oriental Orthodox Churches|Oriental Orthodox]])|value4=3.7|color4=Turquoise|label5={{W|Mythologies of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous}} beliefs|value5=0.7|color5=Chartreuse|label7=[[w:Hinduism|Hinduism]]|value7=0.3|color7=Orange|label8=[[w:Buddhism|Buddhism]]|value8=0.1|color8=Yellow|label6=[[w:Jewish people|Jewish]]|value6=0.1|color6=Hotpink|label9=Others/undeclared|value9=0.6|color9=Black}}Confederal law bans the establishment of an {{W|state religion|official religion}} at any levels of administration, and prevents the government from involving itself in the private and personal religious belief of all people. While it protects the right to believe what a person, group, or {{W|religion}} wishes, it does not allow the right to practice the religion or belief openly and outwardly in a public manner, a central facet of {{W|religious freedom}}. Since the implementation of {{W|secularism}}, the Baliscan government has co-opted the Cerdanyan principle of ''{{W|laïcité|laïcitat}}'', in which the administrations do not recognize any official ''religion''. Instead, it merely recognizes certain religious organizations, according to formal legal criteria that do not address religious doctrine. In return, religious organizations are to refrain from involvement in the social and political policy-making or any activity that could be interpreted as political in a court of law.
[[File:Catharist church interior.png|left|thumb|288x288px|Interior of the Església de [[Talaserra]], constructed by Catharist settlers in [[Cadaqués]] in 1696.]]
In Balisca, "freedom of religious belief" is used as a more broad replacement for both "freedom of religion" and "freedom from religion". The term "belief" is considered inclusive of all forms of {{W|irreligion}}, including {{W|atheism}}, {{W|antitheism}} and {{W|humanism}}.The practice of religion is now legally considered a private matter throughout society and the state.

[[Catharism]] was introduced in the Baliscan archipelago following the arrival of Occitan-speaking explorers and colonists with the permission of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Crown of Volisania]] in the late 15th to early 16th century following the [[Great Disappointment]]. Earlier, these Cathars had been given refugee in Volisania as the faced suspicion, violence and oppression in Western Illypnia.

Compared to other areas of Balisca, very few enslaved Kaftians and Muslims were brought to Cerdanya following the beginning of the [[Kaftian slave trade]]. Historically, an estimated 60% of slaves brought to Balisca from [[West Kaftia|West]]/[[Central Kaftia]] were Muslims. They were overwhelmingly literate in contrast to many of the slave owners. Tensions within Cerdanyan colonial society resulted in the [[Captaincy General of Cerdanya]] outlawing [[W:Chattel slavery|chattel slavery]] in 1611. Following the outbreak of the [[Maroon Wars|Maroon conflict]], a sizable number of runaway slaves were able to flee to Cerdanya, where they were immediately declared free.

The history of irreligion in Cerdanya dates back to 19th century {{W|freethought}} and saw additional developments during the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. A majority of people in the country are irreligious or otherwise unaffiliated, and the country is considered to be one of the most irreligious in the world.

According to the Baliscan Center for Sociological Research, 22.8% of Cerdanyans citizens self-identify as [[Catharism|Catharist]], (18.5% define themselves as not practising, while 4.3% as practising), 3.7% as other [[w:Christianity|Christian]]s (any denomination), 22.4% as followers of other faiths (including {{W|Islam}}, {{W|African diaspora religions}}, {{W|Mythologies of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous beliefs}}, {{W|Hinduism}}, {{W|Buddhism}} etc.), and 62.6% identify as {{W|atheists}} (30.1%), [[W:Agnostics|agnostics]] (20.3%) or non-believers (10.2%) as of January 2022. According to the same poll, the proportion of the unaffiliated is higher among the youth, about 89% among the 20-years old.

==Culture==
{{Main|Culture of Balisca}}

[[File:Théodore Valerio, Pâtre valaque de Zabalcz, Romanian shepherd from Zăbalț, 1852.jpg|thumb|293x293px|''El Rodamón'' ("The Vagabond") painting, by [[W:Théodore Valerio|Théodore Valerio]]. The ''rodamón'' is considered the national personification of Cerdanya.]]
Historically, Cerdanya's culture has been greatly influenced by that of Volisanian [[Catharism|Cathars]], who were originally prominent in Cerdanya's music, architecture, attire, cuisine, and lifestyle. However with Balisca's confederalization, Cerdanya and the other Baliscan communities gradually adopted a [[Cosmocazo|cosmopolitan identity]], aided by [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration from abroad]]. It was largely influenced by Volisanian, Beron and other Illypnian immigration from [[Hersatia]], [[Echia]], [[Sarta]], [[Jacan]], and [[Breisland]] among others. Contemporary Cerdanyan culture is characterized by its idiosyncratic and {{W|permissive society|permissive}} nature, and is often viewed as a the blueprint for Balisca as a whole.

'''''Rodamóns''''' (literally "wanderer" or "vagabond") is a term that was utilized in the 16th century to refer to the Volisan Cathars that were subjected to [[W:Massacre|massacres]] and forced expulsions, first from around the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]], and then again from the [[Estuaria|Estuarian Wetlands]] by Illypnian Catholics during the [[Reapers' War|violent]] [[Colonial Balisca|early colonization of Balisca]]. It was applied with a negative classist connotation to these often poor refugees who were forced to flee north. 

During the [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction era]] and subsequent industrialization, the term saw a resurgence in popularity, being used as a source of pride in the community's "[[W:Rags to riches|rags to riches]]" history. Hence, despite its defects, the ''rodamón'' is considered a [[w:National personification|figure of national identity]] and pride in Cerdanya, and often plays a central role in nationalistic films and speech.
