===Cuisine===
[[File:Pâté_de_pommes_de_terre.JPG|left|thumb|250x250px|A slice of [[W:Pastís de treflas|pastís de treflas]], a kind of potato pie]]
Cerdanya cuisine is largely derived from of [[W:Mediterranean cuisine|Mesogean cuisine]], very close to [[W:Occitan cuisine|Beron]] and [[W:Catalan cuisine|Volisanian cuisine]] with some minor [[W:Italian cuisine|Monsoran]] influences. This is due not only to the great breadth of ethnic diversity of colonial Cerdanya, but to its great geographic diversity as well, both of which grants Cerdanyan cuisine many different kinds of ingredients and dishes adapted to differing localities.

As with other Mesogean cuisines, it has basic ingredients with strong flavors, such as [[W:Garlic|garlic]], [[W:Olive|olives]], salted [[W:Fish|fish]], [[W:Olive oil|olive oil]] and [[W:Wine|wine]], but it also shares some characteristics with Aethiopian cuisines, such as the use of [[W:Cheese|cheese]] and [[W:Butter|butter]]. Some of its specialties are well known around the Mesogean, where they exist in exactly the same form, otherwise with little variation — for example, [[W:Bouillabaisse|bolhabaissa]], [[W:Aioli|alhòli]], [[W:Ratatouille|ratatolha]], and [[W:Pan golçat|pan golçat]] (bread with garlic), with emphasis on [[W:Fines herbes|fines herbes]]. Other specialties include [[W:Cassoulet|caçolet]], [[W:Pastís de treflas|pastís de treflas]], [[W:Aligot|aligòt]], [[W:Farcidures|farcidures]] (potato and meat dumpling), [[W:Freginat|freginat]] (fricassee of pork), [[W:Clafoutis|clafotís]], [[W:Flaugnarde|flaunharda]], and [[W:Garbure|garbure]].

